```java
if (!isCancelled) {
  throw t;
}
throw t;
```

Both the `then` branch and the fall-through unconditionally execute `throw t`, so the `if (!isCancelled)` branch is pure dead control flow. The entire block reduces to a single `throw t;`.

Why behavior-preserving: whichever way the condition evaluates, the exact same statement (`throw t`) runs with the same `t`, so control flow and the thrown object are unchanged. The only thing removed is a read of the `isCancelled` field whose boolean result is never consumed.

Caveat (stated transparently per your exclusions): `isCancelled` is `volatile` (declared line 107). The removed read discards its result and nothing downstream depends on it, so there is no observable memory-visibility effect — but since your rules forbid touching volatile reads, flag this for a human decision. If that makes it out of scope, it still documents a genuine redundancy. Safety of the pure logic change itself is maximal.

**2. Intra-file duplicated `try/catch` for setting thread priority — `DecodeJob.java`, lines 364-376 and 491-501** (category: intra-file-duplication)

The `try/catch` in `restoreThreadPriority()`:
```java
try {
  Process.setThreadPriority(Process.myTid(), GlideExecutor.DEFAULT_PRIORITY);
} catch (IllegalArgumentException | SecurityException e) {
  glideThreadPriorityOverride = null;
  if (Log.isLoggable(TAG, Log.VERBOSE)) {
    Log.v(TAG, "Failed to set thread priority; using default priority for any subsequent jobs.", e);
  }
}
```
is byte-for-byte identical to the block inside `decodeFromRetrievedData()` except for the priority argument (`GlideExecutor.DEFAULT_PRIORITY` vs `glideThreadPriorityOverride.get().intValue()`). Both assign the same field (`glideThreadPriorityOverride = null`) and log the same message.

Why behavior-preserving: the two bodies differ only by a plain `int` value, so they consolidate into `private void trySetThreadPriority(int priority) { ... }` with the value passed as a parameter — no change to which method/field is called or assigned, no callback/functional interface, and no new per-call allocation (an `int` argument boxes nothing). Each call site keeps its own distinct guard conditions, which remain inline.

Note: this is experimental thread-priority plumbing, not bitmap/pool/size math and not synchronization/volatile logic, so it is outside the exclusions. Lower-ranked than #1 because it is a multi-line refactor rather than a one-line deletion.

---

